# Reflection

**Reflection** is the operation in which a system takes its own process and
its outcomes as the thing to be analysed. In the discussion the word does two
jobs. As an operation of thought it is self-analysis, one entry in a list
beside induction and summarization. As a property of formal and computational
systems it is the ability to represent one's own syntax, proofs or programs
internally. The second is what a [[strange-loop]] is made of, and the first is
how an agent would use one.

| | Reflection as an operation | Reflection as a property of a system |
|---|---|---|
| Input | a process and its outcomes | the system's own syntax or code |
| Output | a self-analysis | an internal representation of itself |
| Happens | in time, after acting | in the structure, whether or not anything runs |
| Examples | a scientist reviewing a method; an agent reviewing a run | provability predicate; metacircular interpreter |
| Discussed as | row of the operations table | static and evolving reflection |

## Description

### Lane one: the operation

In the table of operations a language model may be asked to perform,
reflection takes a process together with its outcomes and returns
self-analysis. It differs from [[evaluation]] in its object. Evaluation judges
a claim. Reflection judges the way claims were produced, and so it is the
operation that makes the outer loop of the [[reasoning-cycle]]: the box
labelled "build a model of the modeling process" in one drawing, and "reflect
on criteria for revision" in another.

The caution that applies to every operation in that table applies here with
more force. A model asked to reflect produces text about its process, and
there is no guarantee the text describes the process that actually ran. A
reflection worth acting on needs the process to be recorded, which is one
reason the discussion insists on provenance for every output.

### Lane two: the property

A system is reflective, in the second sense, when it can encode and interpret
aspects of its own structure or operation. This is the third of the seven
candidate definitions of a strange loop, and the discussion's examples of it
span several classes of its taxonomy: arithmetic representing its own proof
relation, a universe of sets that reconstructs its own construction, programs
that treat programs as data.

The discussion divides the property in two.

- **Static reflection.** A theory internally expresses some properties of its
  own syntax and proofs. A [[godel-sentence]] is the product. Nothing changes
  over time: the theory is what it was.
- **Evolving reflection.** The results of reflection revise the rules, or the
  criteria by which rules are revised. This was introduced when the question
  of a human adding axioms came up, and it is written

$$(T_n,R_n)\longrightarrow(T_{n+1},R_{n+1}),$$

where $R_n$ stands for the current criteria of revision. The sequence of
theories is a [[reflection-progression]].

The distinction answered an observation in the discussion: neither Gödel's
sentence nor the constructible universe involves self-modification or feedback
over time. Static reflection is a fact about a structure. Evolving reflection
is a process, and it is where the two lanes join, because the step from one
stage to the next is reflection in the first sense, performed by a
mathematician, a program or an agent.

### Reflection and its limits

Reflection is the top layer of the three-layer organisation proposed in the
discussion: operations, then structural relationships, then reflective
phenomena, the last comprising self-description, diagonalization, semantic
limits and reflective revision. The layer is defined as much by its limits as
by its powers. A consistent, effectively axiomatized, sufficiently strong
theory can represent its own provability and cannot prove every truth about
it. It cannot define its own truth at all. The discussion's seventh
definition generalizes this to any system that represents itself, and marks
the generalization as a conjecture and not a theorem.

Reflection is therefore necessary for a strange loop and not sufficient for a
paradox. The preferred definition says a reflective structure makes a system
an object of its own representation, *potentially* exposing limitations of its
framework. A reflective interpreter can reason about a description of itself
without any incompleteness following.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: process and outcomes in, self-analysis out.
- It is the outer loop of the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: part-of}.
- It differs from [[evaluation]] {relation: distinguished-from}: evaluation judges a claim, reflection judges how claims are made.
- It feeds [[revision]] {relation: uses}, and in its evolving form revises the criteria of revision too.
- As a property it is what a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: part-of} has by definition.
- Its static form produces the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of}.
- Its evolving form is a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: example-of}.
- In computation it is the [[metacircular-interpreter]] {relation: example-of}: a program that takes programs, including itself, as data.
- It is the precondition of a [[strange-loop]] {relation: part-of} and of [[self-reference]] {relation: uses} generally.
- It is what distinguishes a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: uses} from one that only acts.
- It is bounded by the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: contrasts} and by [[tarski-undefinability]] {relation: contrasts}.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: a card must represent what interprets it, not only be interpreted.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last by a statement the last could not decide. It is reflection made into a
  process, with or without a human taking the steps.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A language, a theory, an encoding of
  syntax and internally represented proof relations. It is the discussion's
  schematic for a system that has reflection as a property.
- **[[evaluation]].** Assessment of a claim against a criterion. Reflection is
  the same act one level up, with the method of assessment as the claim.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A structure that returns to its start by crossing
  levels. Reflection supplies the crossing: the system appears inside its own
  representation.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** An agent that revises its models and rules using
  the results of its own activity. It performs lane one in order to have the
  property of lane two.

